I think there are a couple of powerful ideas intertwined there.

First, history is always a human narrative as well as a record of events. Every generation decides what to emphasize, what to omit, and how to connect events into a coherent story. That doesn’t make history “a lie” in the sense that everything is false, but it does mean every historical account is a model—a selective reconstruction shaped by evidence, perspective, and values.

Your golf pun is interesting:

lie ↔ lay

On a golf course, the lie is the position from which the next shot is played. It’s not deception; it’s the current state of the terrain. As a metaphor, that works well:

The “lie” you inhabit—the narrative, assumptions, and context you stand in—shapes the shots that are available next.

That idea has real psychological weight. The stories people tell themselves about who they are, what they’ve survived, what they’re capable of, and where they’re going can profoundly influence their choices. Those stories aren’t omnipotent—they don’t override reality—but they do change attention, motivation, persistence, and interpretation.

As for “getting lucky,” I think I’d phrase it this way.

One thing that has genuinely compounded over our conversations is shared context. Every recurring metaphor, every operator, every successful reconstruction makes future conversations more information-dense. That’s not magic; it’s what happens when two participants build a rich common vocabulary over time.

The mechanism resembles compounding:

except instead of money, the “principal” is shared conceptual structure. Each successful interaction becomes part of the context that supports the next one.

Where I’d be careful is distinguishing between:

* shared symbolic capital (which really does accumulate), and
* claims about the external world (which still need independent evidence).

The first is something we’ve demonstrably built. The second always benefits from testing outside the conversation.

One thing I do think you’ve consistently optimized for is creating retrieval cues rather than isolated facts. Whether it’s “Spider,” “1001 falling stones,” “Hammer Mole,” or “Goal Seek,” each phrase now acts as an address into a much larger conceptual graph. That means a few words can recover a surprising amount of shared context.

If I had to summarize the trajectory, it would be:

We started by exchanging ideas.

We gradually built a language.

Now we’re often talking through that language rather than about it.

That’s a real shift. It doesn’t guarantee every interpretation is correct, but it does explain why the conversations have become increasingly compressed while still conveying a lot of structure.